Subject: How to test for significance of a group-specific negative residual variance in an MG-CFA?
Message-ID: <QynMaTrXXTs.TLElJFv0BAAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/QynMaTrXXTs/m/TLElJFv0BAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear everyone,
After confirming scalar invariance for my ordinal MG-CFA model, I was attempting to estimate latent factor scores, but got one negative residual variance that forbids me from doing so. My dataset contains n = ~31,000 and 14 groups, for model:
```
model <- ' y1 =~ x1 + x2
                  y2 =~ x3 + x4 '
```
wich I call with:

```
mgcfa_scalarfit <- cfa(cfamodel, ordered = TRUE, data = df, group = "countrynameyear", group.equal = c("loadings", "thresholds", "intercepts"), std.lv <http://std.lv> = TRUE)
```
For my eleventh group, labelled `New Zealand_2017`, residual variance of variable x1 came negative. I understand this is a Heywood case, and that I should compare the standard model with a model where the residual variance has been constrained to 0 (or between 0 and 1?), and check whether the difference in fit between the two models is significant. As per my understanding, lavWaldtest() would be a correct tool to check for this.

However, I am struggling to identify how to constrain the model for the residual variance of x1 to be 0.  I attempted:
```
constraint <- '   ancestry ~~ c(NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, z11, NA, NA, NA) * ancestry '
z11 == 0
```
and
```
constraint <- '   ancestry ~~ c(NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, 0, NA, NA, NA) * ancestry '
```
but neither did this work to constrain the residual variance when inserting it directly within the code of the model, nor within the constraints = argument in lavWaldtest().
Therefore, does anybody have any particular idea what I am doing wrong or any suggestion on how to actually do it? That would be life saving (or near).
Thank you!

