In-Reply-To: <jA0FHUXN0kE.0OiK6AitCQAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <jA0FHUXN0kE.0OiK6AitCQAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/jA0FHUXN0kE/m/5ArRjjuyCQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Classical Test Theory item difficulties are the proportion of correct responses in your sample (not the SD of that proportion, watch out for that). They are a "raw measure" of difficulty, if you want to see it like that.

Item thresholds in CFA correspond to difficulty parameters in Item Response Theory, under the following conditions:

- Latent variables are (equally) standardized. In your case, as you have (by default) fixed the factor loading of A_1 to 1, the latent variable of "kon" is not standardized. You can change this default by calling `cfa()` with argument `std.lv <http://std.lv> = TRUE`.
- The parameterization of both models is equal. You can change the default ("theta" parameterization) by calling `cfa()` with argument `parameterization = "delta"`. This way it gives you the values in the "logistic" metric, as you would expect in an IRT model.

Then you can try fitting (e.g. with package {mirt}) a 2-parameter logistic IRT model (with standardized latent variance), and check whether thresholds and item difficulties match.

What you can try with what you have is correlating the thresholds with the CTT item difficulties; you will probably find a relatively high (negative*) correlation.

Hope that helps!

*It should be negative because CTT "item difficulty", as it the proportion of correct responses, is higher the easier the item is. So in fact it should be understood as a measure of "item easiness".

--
Daniel Morillo, Ph.D.
GitHub <https://github.com/DaniMori> |  ORCID <https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3021-3878>

